{
  "abstract": "We study four-vertex separations in a biplanar graph of minimum order among those that are not $q$-colorable, for any $q\\ge4$. Let $X,Y$ be proper induced sides with common root set $S$, and fix a planar cover $P\\cup T$. We prove that if $T[X]+K_4[S]$ is planar, then the exterior union $G[V(X)\\setminus S]$ is connected. A rooted $K_4$ in $T[Y]$ is a sufficient condition, but the theorem requires only the exterior completion and places no bound on side order, density, root edges or degrees. The proof first selects useful root-color partitions by adding edges to a smaller auxiliary side. Planar barriers classify the surviving root-block arrangements in the other layer. Absorbing an entire component of the exterior union then creates a reducible arrangement, while the completion persists by vertex deletion. In the final disconnected-root case, two enlarged auxiliary sides force the same root partition; their colorings combine after discarding one coloring on their shared private vertices. This extends earlier common-block reductions to a connectivity conclusion for arbitrary root-side connectivity. The universal proof is mathematical. Six standard-library verification programs replay small literal controls for the operations and for limitations of stronger geometric assertions. The result restricts a supplied separation; it does not establish that every minimum counterexample contains such a separator, and it does not resolve the connected-exterior case or the Earth--Moon problem.",
